% Test cases for the glucose monitors policy (glucose_monitors.le):
% synthetic claims, one or two per criterion of LCD L33822 and per payment
% condition of Policy Article A52464, each stating only what its record
% shows. The queries: whether the claim is payable (the first query, so
% "stage" reads its checklist), whether it is reasonable and necessary, and
% what minimal change would make it payable.

the target language is: prolog.

the knowledge base glucose monitors cases includes these resources:
    glucose_monitors.

the knowledge base glucose monitors cases includes:

query pay is:
    which claim is payable.

query rn is:
    which claim is reasonable and necessary.

query stage is:
    the query fails at which section.

query flip is:
    which minimal change to the scenario makes it the case that
        claim 1 is payable.

% A complete claim for a home blood glucose monitor (E0607, a purchase): the
% diabetes diagnosis, the practitioner's conclusion on training, the order,
% delivery.
scenario monitor_complete is:
    claim 1 is for the glucose monitor.
    the HCPCS code of the glucose monitor is "E0607".
    the glucose monitor is furnished to Ann.
    the residence of Ann is private residence.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    the diagnosis code of Ann is "E11.9".
    the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the order for the glucose monitor is dated 2026-02-20.
    the order for the glucose monitor is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the glucose monitor includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the glucose monitor includes order date.
    the order for the glucose monitor includes description of the item.
    the order for the glucose monitor includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the glucose monitor includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the glucose monitor was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-22.
    the glucose monitor was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the glucose monitor.
    pay expects answers ["claim 1 is payable"].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers [].

% A diagnosis code outside the Policy Article's list (hypertension, I10):
% basic criterion 1 fails.
scenario no_diabetes_diagnosis is:
    claim 1 is for the glucose monitor.
    the HCPCS code of the glucose monitor is "E0607".
    the glucose monitor is furnished to Ben.
    the residence of Ben is private residence.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    the diagnosis code of Ben is "I10".
    the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about Ben is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    rn expects answers [].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at question"].

% Nothing says the practitioner concluded that Cy is trained: the answer is
% conditional on that finding.
scenario training_not_recorded is:
    claim 1 is for the glucose monitor.
    the HCPCS code of the glucose monitor is "E0607".
    the glucose monitor is furnished to Cy.
    the diagnosis code of Cy is "E10.9".
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"] and unknowns ["the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about Cy is established"].

% Test strips within and over the usual utilization: 100 strips (2 units)
% for three months for Ann, not insulin-treated, are within it; 200 strips
% (4 units) are not, and no visit supports the excess; 300 strips for Dee,
% who is insulin-treated, are within hers.
scenario strips_usual_utilization is:
    claim 1 is for the test strips.
    the HCPCS code of the test strips is "A4253".
    the test strips is furnished to Ann.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 2.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 3 months.
    claim 2 is for the extra test strips.
    the HCPCS code of the extra test strips is "A4253".
    the extra test strips is furnished to Ann.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 4.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 3 months.
    claim 3 is for the insulin user strips.
    the HCPCS code of the insulin user strips is "A4253".
    the insulin user strips is furnished to Dee.
    the quantity billed on claim 3 is 6.
    claim 3 is for a supply of 3 months.
    the diagnosis code of Ann is "E11.9".
    the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the diagnosis code of Dee is "E10.9".
    the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about Dee is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    Dee is currently being treated with insulin.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 3 is reasonable and necessary"].

% High utilization with every requirement: 400 strips (8 units) for three
% months for Eve, insulin-treated, testing six times a day; the visit six
% weeks before the order, the testing log in the record, the order and
% delivery.
scenario high_utilization_documented is:
    claim 1 is for the test strips.
    the HCPCS code of the test strips is "A4253".
    the test strips is furnished to Eve.
    the residence of Eve is private residence.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-05.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 8.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 3 months.
    the diagnosis code of Eve is "E10.9".
    the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about Eve is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    Eve is currently being treated with insulin.
    the treating practitioner had an in-person or telehealth visit with Eve to evaluate diabetes control on 2026-01-15.
    the testing frequency of Eve is corroborated by testing log.
    the order for the test strips is dated 2026-03-01.
    the order for the test strips is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the test strips includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the test strips includes order date.
    the order for the test strips includes description of the item.
    the order for the test strips includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the test strips includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the test strips was communicated to the supplier on 2026-03-02.
    the test strips was delivered on 2026-03-05.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the test strips.
    pay expects answers ["claim 1 is payable"].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers [].

% The same claim without the testing log or a narrative statement of the
% testing frequency: reasonable and necessary, but the Policy Article's
% documentation requirement fails at the remedy; the flip asks for either.
scenario high_utilization_undocumented is:
    claim 1 is for the test strips.
    the HCPCS code of the test strips is "A4253".
    the test strips is furnished to Eve.
    the residence of Eve is private residence.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-05.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 8.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 3 months.
    the diagnosis code of Eve is "E10.9".
    the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about Eve is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    Eve is currently being treated with insulin.
    the treating practitioner had an in-person or telehealth visit with Eve to evaluate diabetes control on 2026-01-15.
    the order for the test strips is dated 2026-03-01.
    the order for the test strips is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the test strips includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the test strips includes order date.
    the order for the test strips includes description of the item.
    the order for the test strips includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the test strips includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the test strips was communicated to the supplier on 2026-03-02.
    the test strips was delivered on 2026-03-05.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the test strips.
    pay expects answers [].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers ["the query fails at remedy"].
    flip expects changes [["add: the testing frequency of Eve is corroborated by narrative statement"], ["add: the testing frequency of Eve is corroborated by testing log"]].

% The practitioner's visit was eight months before the order: criterion (b)
% fails, the excess quantity is not reasonable and necessary.
scenario high_utilization_stale_visit is:
    claim 1 is for the test strips.
    the HCPCS code of the test strips is "A4253".
    the test strips is furnished to Eve.
    the quantity billed on claim 1 is 8.
    claim 1 is for a supply of 3 months.
    the diagnosis code of Eve is "E10.9".
    the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about Eve is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    Eve is currently being treated with insulin.
    the treating practitioner had an in-person or telehealth visit with Eve to evaluate diabetes control on 2025-07-01.
    the testing frequency of Eve is corroborated by testing log.
    the order for the test strips is dated 2026-03-01.
    rn expects answers [].

% A complete claim for a non-adjunctive CGM (E2103) for Fay, who is
% insulin-treated: the diagnosis, the training conclusion, the FDA
% indications, the visit seven weeks before the order, the order, delivery.
% Test strips billed for her in addition (claim 2) are denied.
scenario cgm_insulin_treated is:
    claim 1 is for the CGM.
    the HCPCS code of the CGM is "E2103".
    the CGM is furnished to Fay.
    the residence of Fay is private residence.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-03-01.
    claim 2 is for the test strips.
    the HCPCS code of the test strips is "A4253".
    the test strips is furnished to Fay.
    the quantity billed on claim 2 is 2.
    claim 2 is for a supply of 3 months.
    the diagnosis code of Fay is "E10.9".
    the finding trained on continuous glucose monitor about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding trained on blood glucose monitor about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the CGM is prescribed in accordance with its FDA indications for use.
    Fay is currently being treated with insulin.
    the treating practitioner had an in-person or telehealth visit with Fay to evaluate diabetes control on 2026-01-10.
    the order for the CGM is dated 2026-02-20.
    the order for the CGM is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the CGM includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the CGM includes order date.
    the order for the CGM includes description of the item.
    the order for the CGM includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the CGM includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the CGM was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-22.
    the CGM was delivered on 2026-03-01.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the CGM.
    pay expects answers ["claim 1 is payable"].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary"].
    stage expects answers [].

